readings.c1s1:
¶
'''thunder-stroke†''' (thun′dėr-strōk), ''n.'' A thunder-clap; a stroke or blast by lightning.

{{smaller block|They fell together all, as by consent;<br>They dropp'd as by a ''thunder-stroke.''
{{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, ii. 1. 204.}}}}

'''thunderstruck''' (thun′dėr-struk), ''a.'' '''1.''' Struck, blasted, or injured by lightning.

{{smaller block|''Thunder-struck'' Enceladus,<br>Groveling beneath the incumbent mountain's weight.
{{right|''Addison'', Imit. of Milton, tr. of Story out of the Third [Æneid.}}}}

2. Astonished; amazed; struck dumb by some surprising or terrible thing suddenly presented to the mind or view.

{{smaller block|3 ''Merch.'' I am amazed!<br>1 ''Merch.'' I ''thunderstrook!''
{{right|''Massinger'', Believe as you List, i. 2.}}}}

'''thunder-thump†''' (thun′dėr-thump), ''n.'' A thunderbolt. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|O thou yat throwest the ''thunderthumps''<br>From Heauens hye to Hell.
{{right|''Googe'', Eglogs (ed. Arber), iv.}}}}

'''thunder-tube''' (thun′dėr-tūb), ''n.'' A fulgurite.

'''thunder-worm''' (thun′dėr-wėrm), ''n.'' An amphisbænoid lizard of Florida, ''Rhineura floridana'': so called as forced out of its burrows by a thunder-shower.

'''thundery''' (thun′dėr-i), ''a.'' [Formerly also ''thundry''; < ''thunder'' + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1†.''' Thunder-like; thundering; loud; resounding.

{{smaller block|As a cannon's ''thundry'' roaring ball,<br>Batt'ring one turret, shakes the next withall,<br>And oft in armies (as by proof they finde)<br>Kills oldest souldiers with his very winde.
{{right|''Sylvester'', tr. of Du Bartas. (''Latham.'')}}}}

'''thurify''' (thū′ri-fī), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''thurified'', ppr. ''thurifying''. [< LL. ''thurificare'', ''turificare'', burn incense, < L. ''thus'' (''thur-''), ''tus'' (''tur-''), incense, + ''facere'', make (see ''-fy'').] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To perfume with odors as from a thurible; cense.

{{smaller block|This Herring, or this cropshin, was sensed and ''thurified'' in the smoake.
{{right|''Nashe'', Lenten Stuffe (Harl. Misc., VI. 176).}}
The Smoak of Censing, Smoak of ''Thurifying''<br>Of Images.
{{right|''Sylvester'', Tobacco Battered.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To scatter incense; cense.

'''Thuringian''' (thū-rin′ji-an), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ''Thuringia'' (= G. ''Thüringen'') + ''-an''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Pertaining to Thuringia, a region in central Germany. {{smaller|Properly it is the district included between the Harz, the Thuringian Forest, and the rivers Werra and Saale; but it is often regarded as comprising the Saxon duchies, the principalities of Schwarzburg and Reuss, inclosed exclaves of other states, and adjoining parts of Prussia. Thuringia was a medieval landgraviate, and its later history is merged in that of Saxony.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' A native or an inhabitant of Thuringia.

'''thuringite''' (thū-rin′jīt), ''n.'' [< ''Thuringia'' (see ''Thuringian'') + ''-ite''<sup>2</sup>.] In ''mineral.'', a hydrous silicate of iron and aluminium, occurring as an aggregate of minute scales which are distinctly cleavable in one direction, and have an olive-green color and nacreous luster.

'''thurl, thurling.''' See ''thirl''<sup>1</sup>, ''thirling''.

'''thurm''' (thėrm), ''v. t.'' In ''cabinet-making'', to work (moldings or the like) across the grain of the wood with saw and chisel, thus producing, in square uprights and the like, patterns similar to those turned by the lathe.

