{{right|''MS. Lincoln'' A. i. 17, f. 305. (''Halliwell.'')}}}}

'''thure†''', ''n.'' [< L. ''thus'' (''thur-''), ''tus'' (''tur-''), incense: see ''thus''<sup>2</sup>.] Frankincense.

{{smaller block|An unce of mascul ''thure''<br>Wel smellyng, and an unce of pepur dure.
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 201.}}}}

'''thurgh†.''' A Middle English form of ''thorough'', ''through''<sup>1</sup>, ''through''<sup>2</sup>.

'''thurghfare†''', ''n.'' A Middle English form of ''thoroughfare''.

'''thurghout†''', ''prep.'' A Middle English form of ''thoroughout'', ''throughout''.

'''thurible''' (thū′ri-bl), ''n.'' [< L. ''thuribulum'', ''turibulum'', a censer, < ''thus'' (''thur-''), ''tus'' (''tur-''), frankincense; cf. Gr. θύος, incense, < θύειν, sacrifice; Skt. ''dhūma'', L. ''fumus'', smoke (see ''fume'').] A censer. {{smaller|There is no difference in the meaning of ''thurible'' and ''censer'', except that the former is the more technical ecclesiastical word.}}

{{smaller block|Sweet incense from the waving ''thurible''<br>Rose like a mist.
{{right|''Southey.''}}}}

'''thurifer''' (thū′ri-fėr), ''n.'' [< L. ''thurifer'', ''turifer'', < ''thus'' (''thur-''), ''tus'' (''tur-''), incense, + ''ferre'' = E. ''bear''<sup>1</sup>.] An acolyte who carries the censer.

'''thuriferous''' (thụ̄-rif′ẹ-rus), ''a.'' [< ''thurifer'' + ''-ous.''] Producing or bearing frankincense.

'''thurificate''' (thụ̄-rif′i-kāt), ''a.'' [< LL. ''thurificatus'', ''turificatus'', pp. of ''thurificare'', ''turificare'', burn incense: see ''thurify''.] Having offered incense.—'''The thurificate''', {{smaller|in the ''early church'', those who had offered incense to pagan deities. They formed part of}}

'''thurruck''' (thur′uk), ''n.'' [A further var. of ''thurrough'', itself a var. of ''furrow'', or else a var. and particular use of ''thurrock''. The AS. ''thurruc'' defined by Lye as a canal or drain (''canalis''), does not appear to have had that sense: see ''thurrock''.] A drain. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

'''Thursday''' (thėrz′dā), ''n.'' [< ME. ''Thursday'', ''Thursdey'', ''Thors day'', ''Thores day'', a contracted form (after the Icel. ''Thōrsdagr'') of early ME. ''Thunres dæi'' (which would reg. give mod. E. *''Thundersday''), < AS. ''Thunres dæg'' = OFries. ''Thunresdi'', ''Dunrisdei'', ''Tongeresdei'', ''Tornsdei'' = D. ''Donderdag'' = MLG. ''Donerdach'' = OHG. ''Donarestag'', MHG. ''Donerstac'', G. ''Donnerstag'' = Icel. ''Thōrsdagr'' = Sw. Dan. ''Torsdag;'' orig. two words, ‘Thunder's day,’ ‘Thor's day,’ translating L. ''Dies Jovis:'' see ''thunder'', ''Thor'', and ''day''<sup>1</sup>.] The fifth day of the week. See ''week''. Abbreviated ''Th.'', ''Thur.''—'''Bounds Thursday,''' {{smaller|Ascension day: so called from the old parish custom of marking or beating the bounds. See ''perambulation.''}}—'''Great Thursday, Great and Holy Thursday,''' {{smaller|in the ''Gr. Ch.'', same as ''Maundy Thursday.''}}—'''Green Thursday,''' {{smaller|Thursday in Holy Week; Maundy Thursday.}}—'''Holy Thursday,''' {{smaller|Ascension day: so called because it is the greatest festival of the church year which falls regularly upon a Thursday. This name has always been given to Ascension day in England, both before and since the Reformation. The application of the name to Thursday in Holy Week, properly ''Maundy Thursday'', is recent and incorrect, resting either on confusion or on imitation of foreign (continental) usage.}}—'''Maundy Thursday.''' {{smaller|See ''maundy.''}}—'''Remission Thursday, Sheer Thursday.''' {{smaller|Same as ''Maundy Thursday.''}}—'''Thursday of the Great Canon.''' {{smaller|See ''Great Canon'', under ''great.''}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|Nay, Ellen, blench not ''thus'' away.
{{right|''Scott'', L. of the L., ii. 30.}}}}

(''b'') In the manner just indicated (pointing to something that has just been said, done, or referred to).

{{smaller block|Whether this was a bragge of the Russes or not, I know not, but ''thus'' he sayd.
{{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages'', I. 257.}}}}

{{smaller block|Why hast thou ''thus'' dealt with us?
{{right|Luke ii. 48.}}}}

{{smaller block|The goddess ''thus;'' and thus the god replies,
Who swells the clouds, and blackens all the skies.
{{right|''Pope'', Iliad, viii. 584.}}}}

{{smaller block|Incensed at being ''thus'' foiled, Muley Abul Hassan gave orders to undermine the walls.
{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 44.}}}}

(''c'') In the state or manner now to be indicated (pointing to something immediately following).

{{smaller block|Therein was a record ''thus'' written.
{{right|Ezra vi. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|Were he my kinsman, brother, or my son,
It should be ''thus'' with him; he must die to-morrow.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., ii. 2. 82}}}}

2. Of cause: Consequently; accordingly; so: things being so; hence (pointing to something that follows as an effect).

{{smaller block|''Thus'', for my duty's sake, I rather choose
To cross my friend.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., iii. 1. 17.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Thus'' men are raised by faction, and decried,
And rogue and saint distinguished by their side.
{{right|''Dryden'', The Medal, l. 154.}}}}

3. Of degree or quality: To this extent or proportion; so.

{{smaller block|Whither are you ''thus'' early addrest?
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Catiline, ii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|Even ''thus'' wise—that is, ''thus'' peaceable.
{{right|''Holyday.''}}}}

'''Thus far''', to this point or degree.

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|''Thus far'', with rough and all-unable pen,
Our bending author hath pursued the story.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., Epil.}}}}

'''Thus much''', as much as this; to this extent or degree: as, ''thus much'' by way of apology.

{{smaller block|Onely ''thus much'' now is to be said, that the Comedy is an imitation of the common errors of our life.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Apol. for Poetrie.}}}}

'''thus'''<sup>2</sup> (thus), ''n.'' [L. ''thus'', ''tus'', incense. Cf. ''thurible'', etc.] Frankincense; either (''a'') olibanum or (''b'') the turpentine which concretes on the trunks of the trees yielding turpentine.—'''American thus''', {{smaller|the product chiefly of the long-leaved pine, ''Pinus palustris'', and of the loblolly-pine, ''P. Tæda''.}}

'''thus-gate†''', ''adv.'' [ME., < ''thus''<sup>1</sup> + ''gate''<sup>2</sup>. Cf. ''another-gate''.] In this wise; in this way; thus.

{{smaller block|Now with hym and now with hure and ''thus-gate'' ich begge.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), vi. 51.}}}}

{{smaller block|This is ioyfull tydyng,
That I may nowe here see
The modyr of my lord kyng
''Thus-gate'' come to me.
{{right|''York Plays'', p. 100.}}}}

'''thus-gates†''', ''adv.'' [ME. ''thusgates'', ''thusgatis''; < ''thus-gate'' + adv. gen. ''-es''.] Same as ''thus-gate''.

{{smaller block|To blyse sal I sone be restorede
If I my saule ''thusgates'' wil fede.
{{right|''Political Poems'', etc. (ed. Furnivall), p. 108.}}}}

{{smaller block|And ''thus gatis'' he hailsed the croice.
{{right|''Holy Rood'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 113.}}}}

'''thusness''' (ᴛ̵ʜus′nes), ''n.'' The state of being thus. ''Nature'', XLIII. 435. [Rare except in humorous use.]
